# Build: Organization AI Agent Connector Runtime

Build a Camunda 8.9 **connector runtime** (Spring Boot app) that hosts the **standard, unmodified
Camunda AI Agent connector** (Task + Sub-process) and changes exactly one thing: **how the AWS
Bedrock provider authenticates**. Every Bedrock AI Agent served by this runtime calls an internal
"organization Bedrock gateway" with header credentials (`x-bam-token: <token>`, plus configurable
`Accept` and `Host`) instead of AWS SigV4. All other providers (Anthropic, Azure OpenAI, Vertex AI,
OpenAI, OpenAI-compatible) behave byte-identically to the standard connector.

No fork, no reflection, no AOP, no shading. Integrate only through Camunda's documented
`@ConditionalOnMissingBean` extension point.

## Coordinates and build

- Maven, Java 21, UTF-8, `-parameters`. Maven wrapper (`mvnw`, `mvnw.cmd`, `.mvn/`) checked in.
- `groupId com.anthrobyte.camunda`, `artifactId org-ai-agent-connector-runtime`, `1.0.0-SNAPSHOT`.
- Base package `com.anthrobyte.camunda.aiagent`.
- One property `<version.connectors>8.9.12</version.connectors>` is the single source of truth.
  Import `io.camunda.connector:connector-parent:${version.connectors}` as a BOM
  (`<type>pom</type><scope>import</scope>`) so every transitive version (Spring Boot, LangChain4j,
  AWS SDK, Jackson) comes from Camunda's own tested set. Never pin one yourself except
  `<version.spring-boot>` (must equal the BOM's, used only for the build plugin).
- Dependencies: `io.camunda.connector:spring-boot-starter-camunda-connectors` and
  `io.camunda.connector:connector-agentic-ai`, both at `${version.connectors}` and **scope
  `provided`** (supplied by the Camunda runtime when the thin jar is dropped in, still bundled into
  the fat jar); `spring-boot-configuration-processor` (optional); `spring-boot-starter-test` (test).
- Plugins: compiler 3.15.0 with the configuration-processor annotation path; surefire 3.5.4
  including **both** `**/*Test.java` and `**/*IT.java` (integration tests need no external
  services); jar plugin with `addDefaultImplementationEntries` (version is logged at startup);
  `spring-boot-maven-plugin` `repackage` with `<classifier>exec</classifier>` so the plain jar stays
  thin and the standalone fat jar is `*-exec.jar`.

## How it hooks in

Camunda's `AgenticAiLangchain4JFrameworkConfiguration#langchain4JChatModelFactory` declares
`ChatModelFactory` as `@Bean @ConditionalOnMissingBean`, imported by
`AgenticAiConnectorsAutoConfiguration`. Register our own `ChatModelFactory` from an
`@AutoConfiguration(before = AgenticAiConnectorsAutoConfiguration.class)` so Camunda's default backs
off. Everything else — orchestration, tools, MCP, memory, prompts, job workers, element semantics —
stays Camunda's code.

```
AI Agent → Langchain4JAiFrameworkAdapter → ChatModelFactory
  ◄── OrganizationGatewayChatModelFactory (router)
      ├─ Bedrock   → OrganizationBedrockChatModelBuilder
      │                BedrockRuntimeClient (no signing) + LangChain4j BedrockChatModel
      │                → SdkHttpClient ◄── AuthenticatingSdkHttpClient adds org headers per attempt
      └─ any other → Camunda's ChatModelFactoryImpl, unchanged
```

## Source layout (`src/main/java/com/anthrobyte/camunda/aiagent/`)

### root
- `OrgAiAgentConnectorRuntimeApplication` — plain `@SpringBootApplication` main class.
- `RuntimeStartupReport` — `@Component` on `ApplicationReadyEvent`; logs one line with runtime
  version, connectors version (`AiAgentFunction.class.getPackage().getImplementationVersion()`),
  Java version, active profiles, both AI Agent job types, auth enabled + mode. WARN if auth is
  disabled; WARN if either job type still starts with `io.camunda.agenticai:` (this runtime would
  compete with the default connector runtime for those jobs).

### `auth/` — credentials only; no Camunda, HTTP or AWS dependencies
- `AccessToken(String value, Instant expiresAt)` — record, `toString` prints `[REDACTED]`.
- `AccessTokenSource` — `@FunctionalInterface AccessToken requestToken()`. No caching. Contract in
  javadoc: called by one thread at a time, must never log the token, throws the two exceptions below.
- `GatewayCredentials` — immutable header map (insertion-ordered, non-empty, no blank names or
  values), case-insensitive `containsHeader`, compared by **identity** (invalidation depends on it),
  `toString` prints header **names** only.
- `OrganizationAuthenticationProvider` — `getCredentials()`, `default invalidate(rejected)`,
  `default boolean supportsRefresh() { return false; }`. Thread-safe by contract.
- `AuthenticationFailureReason` — enum with a secret-free `description()`, split into
  permanent (`TOKEN_REQUEST_REJECTED`, `MALFORMED_TOKEN_RESPONSE`, `TOKEN_SOURCE_FAILED`,
  `GATEWAY_REJECTED_CREDENTIALS`, `GATEWAY_ACCESS_DENIED`, `ENDPOINT_NOT_CONFIGURED`) and transient
  (`TOKEN_ENDPOINT_TIMEOUT`, `TOKEN_ENDPOINT_UNREACHABLE`, `TOKEN_ENDPOINT_ERROR`, `INTERRUPTED`).
- `OrganizationAuthenticationException extends dev.langchain4j.exception.NonRetriableException` —
  permanent; LangChain4j never retries it, the AI Agent maps it to `FAILED_MODEL_CALL`.
  `OrganizationAuthenticationUnavailableException extends LangChain4jException` — transient, so
  Camunda's normal job retries apply. Both carry reason + optional HTTP status, **no cause** (a
  cause could carry secrets), and a `copy()` returning a fresh equivalent instance.
- `OrganizationAuthenticationMessages` (package-private) — builds
  `"Organization Bedrock gateway authentication failed: <reason> (HTTP n) [detail]."`, echoing
  `detail` only when it matches `[a-z0-9_ ]{1,64}` (so short machine codes like `invalid_client`
  pass; tokens, JWTs and Base64 never do).
- `PlaceholderJwtTokenSource` — random **unsigned** JWT (`{"alg":"none","typ":"JWT"}`, claims
  `iss=org-ai-agent-connector-runtime`, `sub=placeholder`, `iat`, `exp`, random hex `jti`), base64url
  without padding, trailing dot for the empty signature. Takes `Duration lifetime` + `Clock`. Logs a
  startup WARN that this is a placeholder. Never logs the token.
- `StaticHeadersAuthenticationProvider` — fixed headers, `supportsRefresh() == false`.
- `CachingTokenAuthenticationProvider implements OrganizationAuthenticationProvider, AutoCloseable`
  — the important one:
  - Fast path is **lock-free**: one volatile read of an `AtomicReference<CachedCredentials>`.
  - **Single-flight refresh** behind a `ReentrantLock`; late arrivals double-check and take the
    fresh token instead of issuing their own request (no stampede when many jobs start at once).
  - **Shared failure**: threads that waited on a refresh that failed get a `copy()` of that same
    failure (tracked by a completed-refresh sequence number) rather than each hammering the source.
  - **Bounded waiting**: `tryLock(refreshWaitTimeout)`; timeout → transient `TOKEN_ENDPOINT_TIMEOUT`;
    interruption → re-interrupt + transient `INTERRUPTED`.
  - **Expiry with skew**: valid until `expiresAt - refreshSkew`, skew capped at half the lifetime so
    a short-lived token is never expired on arrival.
  - **Identity-based `invalidate`**: clears only if the rejected object is still current, so a burst
    of concurrent 401s causes one refresh.
  - Header built from `headerName` + `headerValueTemplate` (must contain `{token}`), preceded by the
    configured additional/static headers in order.
  - Any unexpected `RuntimeException` from the source becomes a permanent `TOKEN_SOURCE_FAILED`
    **without its message** (it could hold secrets); only the exception *type* is logged.
  - `close()` drops the cache and closes an optional `AutoCloseable resources` (the token HTTP
    client). Logs refresh counts (a climbing count at short intervals = 401 churn).
- `auth/oauth2/ClientCredentialsSettings` — record (tokenUri, clientId, clientSecret, scope,
  audience, `ClientAuthentication` enum `CLIENT_SECRET_BASIC|CLIENT_SECRET_POST`,
  additionalParameters, requestTimeout, defaultTokenLifetime), secret redacted in `toString`.
- `auth/oauth2/ClientCredentialsTokenClient implements AccessTokenSource` — stateless RFC 6749 §4.4
  request over the JDK `HttpClient`. Form-encoded `grant_type=client_credentials` + optional scope,
  audience and extra params; Basic or POST client auth. Maps: 2xx with `access_token` → `AccessToken`
  (`expires_in`, else `defaultTokenLifetime`); 400/401/403 → permanent `TOKEN_REQUEST_REJECTED`
  surfacing only the OAuth `error` code (`[a-z0-9_]{1,40}`); unparseable body → permanent
  `MALFORMED_TOKEN_RESPONSE`; 429/5xx → transient `TOKEN_ENDPOINT_ERROR`; timeout → transient
  `TOKEN_ENDPOINT_TIMEOUT`; `IOException` → transient `TOKEN_ENDPOINT_UNREACHABLE`. Response bodies
  are never logged or put in messages.

### `transport/` — AWS SDK decorator; no Camunda dependencies
- `AuthenticatingSdkHttpClient implements SdkHttpClient` wrapping the Apache client Camunda would
  build. `prepareRequest` is called by the SDK **once per attempt, after signing**, so this is the
  only correct hook (`ExecutionInterceptor#modifyHttpRequest` runs once per execution, before
  retries).
  - `authorize(...)`: remove any header whose name collides with a credential header and the AWS
    signing headers `authorization`, `x-amz-date`, `x-amz-security-token`, `x-amz-content-sha256`
    (unless the organization itself sends one), then put the credential headers. Method, URI, body,
    content-stream provider and metric collector are preserved unchanged.
  - **Fail closed**: credentials are fetched before anything is sent.
  - **One retry on 401**: always `invalidate(credentials)`; retry once with refreshed credentials
    only if `retryOnUnauthorized` && `supportsRefresh()` && not aborted; discard the first response
    body to release the connection; a second 401 invalidates again and is returned as-is.
  - Every status, including a final 401/403, is returned to the SDK unchanged.
  - `abort()` propagates to the in-flight request. `close()` closes the delegate.
  - Logging: gateway host, method, path, status, durationMs, attempt, header **names**, and a
    request id from `x-amzn-RequestId`/`x-amz-request-id`/`x-request-id`. WARN on 429/5xx, DEBUG on
    401/403 (the retry path and chat model log those with context), INFO otherwise. Never a value.
- `AuthenticatingSdkHttpClientBuilder implements SdkHttpClient.Builder<...>` — wraps the delegate
  builder so it is passed to `BedrockRuntimeClientBuilder#httpClientBuilder` (not `#httpClient`),
  letting the SDK own and close the client, exactly as with Camunda's own builder.

### `camunda/` — the only Camunda integration points
- `OrganizationGatewayChatModelFactory implements ChatModelFactory` — thin router. Every
  `BedrockProviderConfiguration` → our builder, **no fallback** to Camunda's Bedrock auth. Anything
  else → an unmodified `ChatModelFactoryImpl` built exactly like Camunda's default bean, logged at
  INFO on purpose (those calls carry no organization credentials).
- `OrganizationBedrockChatModelBuilder` — builds `BedrockRuntimeClient` with
  `region(Region.of(...))`, `endpointOverride(gatewayEndpoint)`, `AnonymousCredentialsProvider`,
  `putAuthScheme(NoAuthAuthScheme.create())` and an `authSchemeProvider` that resolves **only**
  `smithy.api#noAuth` (Bedrock's default provider offers sigv4 and httpBearerAuth; this makes SigV4
  or a bearer token from `AWS_BEARER_TOKEN_BEDROCK` impossible), `apiCallTimeout(timeout)`, and our
  authenticating http-client builder. Then a LangChain4j `BedrockChatModel` (`client`, `modelId`,
  `timeout`, mapped inference config) wrapped in `OrganizationAuthenticatedChatModel` and returned
  as a `CloseableChatModelDelegate(chatModel, client)`.
  - **Endpoint**: the element's "Custom endpoint" is required and used **exactly as configured** —
    any absolute http(s) URL with a host is accepted, no allow-list, no scheme restriction. Missing
    / unparseable / not absolute → permanent `ENDPOINT_NOT_CONFIGURED`, logging scheme, host, port
    and path but never user-info. Never falls back to AWS.
  - WARN when the element carries AWS credentials or an API key: they are ignored.
  - Close the client if model construction throws.
- `CamundaBedrockClientParity` (package-private) — **one class holding everything copied from
  Camunda**, because the originals are private/package-private and cannot be called: the 15 s
  `CONNECTION_TIMEOUT`, `deriveTimeout` (element timeout if positive, else
  `camunda.connector.agenticai.aiagent.chat-model.api.default-timeout`), the Apache builder with
  proxy config + connection/socket timeouts (mirroring `ChatModelHttpProxySupport`), and
  `applyModelParameters` (`maxTokens`→`maxOutputTokens`, `temperature`, `topP`, set only when
  present). Javadoc must name the exact source methods and say "diff these on every upgrade".
- `OrganizationAuthenticatedChatModel implements ChatModel` (package-private) — fetches credentials
  **before** delegating, so a credential failure throws the sanitized exception with nothing sent
  and outside LangChain4j's retry loop. On failure, walks up to 16 causes: unwraps our exceptions as
  fresh `copy()`s; turns a final `SdkServiceException` 401/403 into
  `GATEWAY_REJECTED_CREDENTIALS`/`GATEWAY_ACCESS_DENIED` without the payload; **rethrows everything
  else unchanged** (LLM 4xx, gateway 5xx, timeouts) so Camunda's behaviour is untouched. Logs one
  INFO line per call: model, finishReason, tool calls requested, token usage, message/tool counts,
  durationMs — never content. Delegates all other `ChatModel` methods.

### `config/`
- `OrganizationAuthProperties` — `@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "organization.ai-gateway.auth")`
  as a record. Its own prefix, not `camunda.*`, so a future Camunda release cannot clash. Fields:
  `enabled` (false), `mode` (`PLACEHOLDER_JWT|OAUTH2_CLIENT_CREDENTIALS|STATIC_HEADERS|CUSTOM`,
  default `PLACEHOLDER_JWT`), `retryOnUnauthorized` (true), `allowInsecureHttp` (false),
  `List<Header> staticHeaders`, nested `Token` (headerName `x-bam-token`, headerValueTemplate
  `{token}`, refreshSkew PT60S, refreshWaitTimeout PT15S), `PlaceholderJwt(lifetime PT5M)`,
  `OAuth2(...)`. `Header` and `OAuth2` redact values in `toString`.
  `validate()` collects **every** problem and throws one `IllegalStateException` listing them, always
  naming the property (and the env var that maps to it) and **never a value**: valid header names,
  non-blank values, `Host` must be `host[:port]` not a URL, `{token}` present in the template,
  non-negative skew, positive durations, OAuth2 requires token-uri/client-id/client-secret, the
  token URL must be absolute https (http only with `allowInsecureHttp`) and must not contain
  user-info, `STATIC_HEADERS` needs at least one header.
- `OrganizationAuthAutoConfiguration` —
  `@AutoConfiguration(before = AgenticAiConnectorsAutoConfiguration.class)`,
  `@ConditionalOnBooleanProperty(".enabled")`,
  `@ConditionalOnProperty("camunda.connector.agenticai.framework", havingValue="langchain4j", matchIfMissing=true)`,
  `@EnableConfigurationProperties`. Two beans:
  - `OrganizationAuthenticationProvider` (`@ConditionalOnMissingBean`, so an app can replace the
    whole thing) selected by mode. `CUSTOM` requires an `AccessTokenSource` bean, else fail fast.
    OAuth2 builds a JDK `HttpClient` with `Redirect.NEVER` (the secret must only ever reach the
    configured host) and the same `CONNECTOR_HTTP(S)_PROXY_*` settings the AI Agent uses, and hands
    it to the caching provider as the closeable resource.
  - `ChatModelFactory` overriding Camunda's. Validates, logs mode/headers/provider/refresh support,
    WARNs that any URL an element points at receives the organization credentials, WARNs if
    `allowInsecureHttp`, and WARNs if the `Host` header still ends in `.invalid` (the shipped
    placeholder would otherwise only surface as gateway routing failures).
- Register it in
  `src/main/resources/META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports`.

